gluster-block release 0.5.33
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (32).
Host maintenance: take one host of a block hosting volume out of service
and back without I/O errors on the initiators. All hosts of a volume need
this version for the new commands (capabilities modify_prio and
host_maintenance).

Features:
---------
27. 'gluster-block hosts <volname> [<host>]': state of the hosts of a
    volume read from configfs of every host: gluster-blockd, tcmu-runner,
    loaded blocks, enabled TPGs, prio paths, logged in initiators and
    problems; a dead host is reported after 3 seconds.
28. 'gluster-block modify <volname/blockname> prio-path <host> [force]':
    move the active/optimized ALUA path of a block to another of its
    hosts, put back on all hosts if one fails.
29. 'gluster-block hosts <volname> rebalance [dry-run]': spread the prio
    paths of a volume over the hosts that answer, in background.
30. 'gluster-block hosts <volname> <host> maintenance start [force]' and
    'maintenance stop [force] [rebalance]': move the prio paths off the
    host, disable its TPGs (kept over a reboot), bring it back; create,
    replace and prio-path onto a host in maintenance are refused.
31. maintenance start waits until the initiators do no more I/O through
    the LUNs of the host (GB_MAINT_IO_IDLE_SEC, GB_MAINT_IO_WAIT_SEC)
    and stops if they do not; force asks for a confirmation that lists
    what it skips.

Fixes:
------
32. an RPM upgrade restarted gluster-block-target: 'targetctl clear'
    dropped every iSCSI session of the host. Upgrades (also from 0.5.27)
    now restart only gluster-blockd, on RPM and .deb.


gluster-block release 0.5.27
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (26).

Fixes:
------
25. 'gluster-block --help', '--version' and '--usage' failed with
    "expecting '--json*'".

Documentation:
--------------
26. README: block volumes on dispersed gluster volumes need
    'prealloc full' and 'locks.mandatory-locking optimal', otherwise
    targetcli fails with "UserBackedStorageObject creation failed".


gluster-block release 0.5.25
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (24).
Fixes for the issues left open in gluster/gluster-block.

Fixes:
------
21. configure turned systemd on only when /usr/lib/systemd/system
    existed and ignored --with-systemddir, on Debian the units were not
    installed (gluster/gluster-block#215).
22. --enable-tirpc=no failed late with 'rpc/rpc.h: No such file or
    directory' on glibc >= 2.26, configure now explains it
    (gluster/gluster-block#290).

Features:
---------
23. build on Debian 12 and Ubuntu 22.04/24.04: install_deps.sh, README,
    gluster-block-target.service conflicts with rtslib-fb-targetctl.
24. .deb packages for Debian 12 and Ubuntu 22.04/24.04
    (extras/make_debs.sh), built in GitLab CI (deb:<distro> jobs,
    packages:all) and GitHub Actions.

Known issue: 'gluster-block --help' fails with "expecting '--json*'",
use 'gluster-block help' (fixed in 0.5.27).


gluster-block release 0.5.21
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (20).

Features:
---------
19. 'gluster-block status' shows the step of a running create or
    resize in PHASE, e.g. 'allocating storage (zero filling, 42%)',
    the bytes done in PROGRESS and the estimated end time with the
    time left in FINISHED. The IN-PROGRESS response has no hint line
    any more.
20. new gluster-block-selinux package with a policy module: targetclid
    (confined on EL9) may write the configshell log in
    /var/log/gluster-block. Before every targetcli command failed with
    "Permission denied" when SELinux was enforcing.


gluster-block release 0.5.19
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (18).

Fixes:
------
18. gluster-block-target.service was refused on EL9 ("bad unit file
    setting"): systemd ignores '.include'. The unit repeats the settings
    of target.service now, ExecStop runs through a shell so the
    configuration is really cleared, and target-restore (targetctl) is
    required on EL8+.


gluster-block release 0.5.18
============================

The patch number counts the problems solved since 0.5.1 (17).

Behaviour changes:
------------------
* create and modify size return 'RESULT: IN-PROGRESS' with an
  OPERATION-ID and finish in background. Use 'gluster-block status' to
  track them, or '--wait' (GB_CLI_WAIT=yes) to wait for the result as
  before. Scripts that need the final result must use --wait.
* Other modifying commands fail at once with EBUSY while such an
  operation runs on the volume; list, info, status and genconfig keep
  working.
* create with 'prealloc no' on a dispersed volume asks for
  confirmation, use '--yes' in scripts. Without a terminal it fails
  with exit code 228.

Features:
---------
1. zerofill <yes|no> option for create
2. fallocate for create and resize, ftruncate + zerofill fallback
3. create and resize run in background, new 'status' command, --wait
4. operation results are shared between nodes through /block-ops
5. confirmation for thin blocks on dispersed volumes, --yes
6. builds for CentOS Stream 9, Rocky Linux and AlmaLinux 8/9
7. GitLab CI with per distribution RPM artifacts

Fixes:
------
8. subsequent delete failures
9. data races from non reentrant strtok() in the daemon threads
10. daemon options without an argument swallowed the next option
11. daemon crash on exit (config released before the last log)
12. create reported success when the HA count was not configured
13. inter-node RPC wire format broken by the prealloc/zerofill fields
14. 'prealloc full' left a sparse file without fallocate support,
    zerofill skipped after a successful fallocate
15. resize did not follow the allocation mode of the block
16. zerofill accepted without prealloc or with storage
17. timeout option value read from a fixed argument position


gluster-block release 0.5.1 is tagged.

This is a security and bugfix release.

An information-disclosure flaw was found in the way gluster-block logs
sensitive information. This flaw allows an attacker with access to the
gluster-block logs to read potentially sensitive information, such as
the CHAP passwords for block volumes.

When tuned to debug log-level, gluster-block captutures the targetcli exec
commands output at gluster-blockd.log which might contain sensitive details.
Also block volume create/modify/info cli command outputs might contain
sensitive information, as part of the audit logging these outputs will be
captured at cmd_history.log and gluster-blockd.log (CVE-2020-10762)

Administrators may want to check old logs for gluster-block passwords if they
created block volumes with CHAP authentication enabled. Restrict access or
remove old logs that retain the passwords.

The flaw was discovered and fixed by Prasanna Kumar Kalever of Red Hat.
Refer: https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/CVE-2020-10762

Notable Fixes:
--------------
* Fix CVE-2020-10762
* Fix delete failures when backend file is absent
* Add logo for gluster-block project

Read more at [1] and [2]

[1] https://github.com/gluster/gluster-block/blob/master/README.md
[2] https://github.com/gluster/gluster-block/blob/master/INSTALL

Cheers!
